@page "/setup"
@inject ChallengeConfig Config

<PageTitle>Setup — Santa's Workshop</PageTitle>

<div class="wrap">
    <h1>Get set up</h1>
    <p>
        You need two things: a MongoDB deployment that supports <strong>Search</strong>
        and <strong>Vector Search</strong>, and the <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener">.NET 10 SDK</a>.
        There are three routes to that. Pick one — you don't need all three.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            <strong>Whichever route you pick, finish by running the environment check.</strong>
            It tells you exactly what's wrong and how to fix it, which is a lot faster
            than reading an exception.
        </p>
        <pre><code>dotnet run --project tools/doctor</code></pre>
        <p>Run it again any time something stops working.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Option 1 — GitHub Codespaces</h2>
    <p>Easiest by a distance, and nothing lands on your machine.</p>
    <p>
        On <a href="@Config.Repo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the repo</a>, choose
        <strong>Code → Codespaces → Create codespace on main</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
        .NET 10, MongoDB and the sample data are all set up for you, and the environment
        check runs by itself once the container has finished building. The first build
        takes a few minutes; after that it's seconds.
    </p>

    <h2>Option 2 — Local, with Docker</h2>
    <p>
        You need <a href="https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker Desktop</a>
        and the .NET 10 SDK.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git clone @(Config.Repo).git
cd @Config.RepoName

docker compose up -d     # MongoDB, with Search and Vector Search
docker compose ps        # wait for "healthy" — about 20 seconds

dotnet run --project tools/doctor</code></pre>
    <p>
        That's the whole setup. The connection string is
        <code>mongodb://workshop:workshop@@localhost:27017/?directConnection=true</code>,
        and the code finds it on its own — there's nothing to configure.
    </p>

    <h2>Option 3 — Atlas free tier</h2>
    <p>
        Use this if Docker isn't an option, or you'd rather work against the real thing.
        The free M0 tier covers every part of this challenge.
    </p>
    <p>
        Create a cluster at
        <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/cloud/atlas/register" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mongodb.com/cloud/atlas/register</a>,
        add a database user, add your IP to the access list, copy the C# connection
        string, then point the code at it:
    </p>
    <pre><code>export MONGODB_URI="mongodb+srv://USER:PASSWORD@@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/"
dotnet run --project tools/doctor</code></pre>
    <p>
        If the doctor can't reach it, it's nearly always the IP access list — your
        home IP moves, and café wifi certainly does.
    </p>

    <h2>API keys</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>You need no keys at all for Parts 1, 2 and 3A.</strong> Don't go and
        collect them yet. When you reach Part 3B, you'll want these — both free, and
        neither asks for a card:
    </p>

    <ul class="links">
        <li>
            <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/voyageai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VOYAGE_API_KEY — Voyage AI ↗</a>
            <span>Parts 3B and 4. Turns text into vectors. Part 3B also ships pre-embedded data, so you can skip this if you'd rather.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="https://aistudio.google.com/apikey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GEMINI_API_KEY — Google AI Studio ↗</a>
            <span>Part 4 only. The language model behind the elf assistant.</span>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>Set them as environment variables, or as .NET user secrets so they survive a rebuild:</p>
    <pre><code>dotnet user-secrets set "GEMINI_API_KEY" "..."</code></pre>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            <strong>Never commit a key.</strong> Nothing in this repo ever asks you to
            paste one into a source file, and <code>.env</code> is already ignored by git.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>When something breaks</h2>
    <p>
        Start with <code>dotnet run --project tools/doctor</code>. It covers the common
        failures — the server not running, bad credentials, a standalone server instead
        of a replica set, missing keys — and tells you what to do about each.
    </p>
    <p>
        One gotcha worth knowing in advance: <strong>search indexes take about twenty
        seconds to become queryable</strong>, and until they are, queries return zero
        results with no error at all. If Part 3A looks like it found nothing, wait
        twenty seconds and try again before you start debugging.
    </p>
    <p>
        The full troubleshooting list lives in
        <a href="@(Config.Repo)/blob/main/docs/setup.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs/setup.md ↗</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="parts">On to the challenges</a>
    </div>
</div>
